WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
Two muscles: adaptability and resilience.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.500
Tuesday, 7:40. Laras opens her inbox. Rejection number nine.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:18.490
At nine, the reporting tool she spent a month learning is gone. The team works in a new one.

4
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:24.090
Two bad surprises in one morning. But they are not the same problem.

5
00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:31.660
The new tool asks her to change her method: adaptability. The rejection drains her energy; getting it back is resilience. Both can be trained.

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:40.530
For the rejection, Laras runs a recovery loop. Four steps, fifteen minutes.

7
00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:48.880
Name it, precisely. Not “I am bad at this”, but “I failed the numerical test at stage two.”

8
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.810
Normalise it. Most applicants are rejected most of the time. This is the terrain, not a verdict on her.

9
00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:00.560
Take one thing she controls. She ran out of time on the last ten questions.

10
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.880
Choose the next step, and put it in the calendar. Twenty minutes of timed practice, tomorrow at eight.

11
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.080
Wednesday, eight o'clock. A timer, ten questions. She still gets two wrong. But this time she finishes.

12
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:19.800
For the new tool, the answer is different. She changes her method: fifteen minutes with a teammate, then one small report rebuilt by herself.

13
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.670
Friday. Laras presents her first report in the new tool. Nobody calls it the new tool any more.

14
00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:33.070
A setback you process becomes data. A setback you avoid becomes fear.

15
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.870
Now it is your turn. Pick one setback from the last month, and run the loop.
